The evaluation found that nurse-led discharge planning:
- shortened hospital stays, with mean length of stay falling from 7.9 to 7.1 days, an adjusted reduction of 0.8 days;
- did not increase 30-day readmissions or deaths;
- slightly improved early discharge, with more patients leaving before noon.

The report’s conclusion was: “Nurse-led discharge planning shortened hospital stays without an increase in readmissions or deaths. The model could be extended to other wards.”

ANSWER: It found that nurse-led discharge planning reduced length of stay without increasing readmissions or deaths, and improved earlier discharge, so it could be extended to other wards.
